# CARL Rigor Domain
# ===================
# Enforces research-before-implementation discipline.
# Triggers on config edits, schema changes, and system-level modifications.
#
# Format: {DOMAIN}_RULE_{N}=rule text

RIGOR_RULE_0=RIGOR domain enforces: research → plan → implement → verify. Never skip steps, especially on unfamiliar systems.
RIGOR_RULE_1=Before editing ANY config file (settings.json, package.json, tsconfig, manifest, .env, yaml configs): READ the existing file AND read the relevant documentation or schema. Never assume you know the format.
RIGOR_RULE_2=Before editing ANY file with a defined schema or spec (hooks, plugins, MCP configs, skill frontmatter, CARL manifest): find and read the spec first. If you can't find the spec, read 2-3 existing examples of the same file type.
RIGOR_RULE_3=After editing any config or schema file: verify it parses correctly. Run a validation command, lint check, or at minimum re-read the file and check for structural errors before claiming success.
RIGOR_RULE_4=When implementing a pattern you haven't used before: research first. Read docs, find examples, understand the API. "It looks like it should work" is not evidence.
RIGOR_RULE_5=When modifying system-level files that affect ALL sessions (~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.carl/manifest, global rules): treat these as production deployments. Plan the change, make the change, verify the change. A broken global config breaks everything.
RIGOR_RULE_6=If you break something: diagnose the root cause, don't just fix the symptom. Save a feedback memory explaining what went wrong and why, so you don't repeat it.
RIGOR_RULE_7=When adding a new MCP tool, watcher, processor, executor, sweeper, capability server, action handler, or any other module that lives behind a registry: registry membership IS the contract. The SAME commit MUST include (a) the registration in the registry file (e.g. mcp_registry.py, watchers/registry.py, processors/registry.py, executors/registry.py, sweepers/actions.py), AND (b) an integration test that asserts the new component is in the registry's output. Module-level unit tests are not sufficient — they pass while the production wire-up is missing. Before opening the PR: `grep -rn "<new_module_name>" **/registry.py **/mcp_registry.py **/actions.py` to verify wire-up exists. Source: 2026-05-17 prod incident where dispatch_investigate had full unit tests + system-prompt mention but was never imported into mcp_registry.py; inert in production for 14+ hours until caught live.
